</div>
<h2 id="backstory">The Notre Dame Backfield</h2>
<p>Love was the headline name in South Bend for three seasons, a unanimous All-American and Doak Walker Award winner who finished his final year with 1,652 yards from scrimmage and 21 touchdowns on a staggering 7.1 yards per carry. Price played behind him for most of that stretch, still managing 1,420 rushing yards at 6.1 yards per carry across his final two seasons as Notre Dame's clear No. 2 back — production that quietly built a first-round case of its own. Arizona took Love third overall in the 2026 draft; Seattle grabbed Price at the very end of the first round, No. 32 overall.</p>
<h2 id="combine">The Combine Numbers</h2>
<p>At the 2026 combine, Love ran a 4.36-second 40-yard dash at 214 pounds — the second-fastest time of any running back in Indianapolis and the fastest ever recorded by a running back with a 90-plus production score over the prior decade. He chose not to participate in the vertical or broad jump, content to let the 40 time and his college tape carry his stock. Price, by contrast, ran a full workout: a 4.49-second 40, a 1.61-second 10-yard split, a 35-inch vertical jump, and a 10-foot-4 broad jump — solid across the board, with the pass-catching drills that followed drawing as much praise as the testing numbers themselves.</p>
