<h2 id="2026">Where They Stand for Week 1</h2>
<p>Love's rookie season opened with real uncertainty. He suffered a high ankle sprain in Arizona's first preseason game, the kind of injury that can linger into the regular season even when it isn't season-ending. As of the start of September, he's described as being in "red-alert mode" to be ready in time — his blazing combine speed doesn't do him much good if the ankle isn't sound enough to plant and cut at full go.</p>
<p>Price's path to a starting workload came through a different door entirely: Seattle's presumed lead back Zach Charbonnet tore his ACL late last season and opened 2026 on the Reserve/PUP list, handing Price the first-team reps as a rookie far earlier than expected. Betting markets have taken notice — Price has drawn some of the heaviest ticket volume of any Offensive Rookie of the Year candidate, priced as a live long shot behind his former teammate.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>The Testing Gap, In Context</strong>
  A 4.36 to 4.49 gap in the 40 is meaningful but not the whole picture — Price's 35-inch vertical and 10'4" broad jump both graded well above the running back average, showing a different kind of explosiveness than Love's straight-line speed. Two backs from the same college backfield rarely test as similarly as fans expect; body type, workload role, and what a team asks a back to do all shape how a testing day plays out.
</div>
